#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074877} :activity/id #uuid "b18f153a-24ae-52c2-833b-f1d51a752952" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050948 :topic/id #uuid "7447137e-4148-57e3-9e8c-8ef64742b913" :topic/math-academy-id 561} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074968 :tutorial/content "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2}),$ $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}),$ $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8}).$\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "05d7bd91-e8b4-5ada-936f-6036f766cf23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2053 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e3c01e56-883c-55ab-9e6a-10ceb0575884" :step/math-academy-id 7803 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073565 :question/id #uuid "9a740a80-f655-5d20-a839-d9a809d4c6a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-2285"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1c72ed5-ab55-5815-b0a2-9a88eb15d362" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{: